WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers episode 57.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.720
It's April 24th, 2026.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.780
Some fascinating developments to cover today.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.720
NASA has released a fresh batch of images

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.940
from Artemis II operations showing Earth,

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.800
the moon and the sun captured across multiple mission phases.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.120
These fresh photographs were taken

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.640
during different operational stages of the flight.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.460
They serve as a detailed visual record

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.320
that mission teams can study closely.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.200
The images help engineers refine procedures

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.740
for everything from navigation to crew activities.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.320
What stands out is how these real visuals

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.400
from deep space bridge the gap between simulation

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.840
and actual flight conditions.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.320
It's a critical step that brings us closer

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.820
to the operational rhythm needed for future crewed lunar flights.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.920
Speaking of crewed flights, NASA just announced

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.180
who will be heading to the International Space Station

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:47.920
later this year.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.500
The agency has named four astronauts

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.140
from three different nations for the crew 13 mission.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.100
They will launch aboard a SpaceX crew dragon in September.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.020
This assignment keeps the ongoing crew rotation

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.520
on schedule aboard the station.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.300
It also strengthens international cooperation

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.580
in the research happening in low Earth orbit.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.960
Having partners from multiple countries working together

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.480
reminds me how much we gain when we share the work

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.620
and the discoveries.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.340
That steady human presence up there

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.560
continues to deliver science that benefits us all back home.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.800
That spirit of international partnership

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.700
is also growing on the lunar side.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.940
Jordan has officially become the 63rd signatory

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.060
to the Artemis Accords.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.720
The country signed the non-binding principles

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.340
that outline responsible exploration of the moon.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.060
This brings the total number of participating nations to 63.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.760
It expands the coalition that is working

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.200
toward a shared framework for all future lunar activities.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.660
I find it encouraging to see more countries formally

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.440
committing to these common guidelines.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.260
It suggests a growing global understanding

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.380
that the moon should be explored thoughtfully and together.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.200
While nations line up behind the Artemis program,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.100
China continues marching forward

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.620
with its own independent lunar ambitions.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.360
Officials in China provided an update

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.820
on their manned lunar plans

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.920
during the country's space day celebrations.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.640
They outlined steady progress on developing

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.360
the capabilities needed for accrued lunar landing.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.920
The presentation highlighted ongoing hardware testing

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.180
and work on the overall mission architecture.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.200
Their aim remains sending Chinese astronauts

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.600
to the lunar surface within the coming decade.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.460
It's a clear demonstration of how multiple nations

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.520
are advancing their exploration roadmaps in parallel.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.020
Watching these different approaches unfold side by side

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.220
makes the coming years feel especially dynamic.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.340
Back on the American side,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.520
hardware for the first lunar landing mission

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.720
is already taking shape.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.800
Engineers at NASA's Marshall Space Flight Center

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.500
gave an exclusive behind-the-scenes update

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.860
on systems being prepared for Artemis III.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.740
The work includes tangible hardware fabrication

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.940
and testing that is happening right now.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.000
This kind of concrete progress shows how the pieces

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.140
for returning humans to the lunar surface are coming together.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.440
Seeing actual components move through assembly bays

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.260
always makes these future missions feel more real to me.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.980
It is satisfying to know the engineering teams

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.660
are turning plans into flight ready equipment every day.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.320
One of the astronauts who has already walked on the moon

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.520
recently shared his thoughts on these new efforts.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.840
Apollo 17 astronaut Harrison Schmidt reflected

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.700
on his own lunar experiences during a recent interview.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.640
As the only geologist who have walked on the moon,

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.960
he connected those moments to today's plans

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.060
for sustained presence.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.920
He spoke about the search for life beyond Earth

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.380
and why a long-term foothold on the moon matters.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.880
It is powerful to hear someone who is there

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.020
bridge the gap between the Apollo era

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.540
and what we are building now.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.360
His perspective adds a human depth that reminds us

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:48.440
exploration is as much about curiosity as it is about technology.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.900
That sense of wonder is clearly reaching

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.280
the next generation as well.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.620
Students at Mandin Middle School have shown strong engagement

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.680
while learning about NASA's Artemis goals.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.080
Their curriculum uses the real missions

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.740
to spark interest in science and engineering topics.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.320
Teachers report the students light up

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.280
when they discuss the idea of sustained lunar exploration.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.340
It is a perfect example of how current programs

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.380
can inspire young people to imagine careers in these fields.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.960
Moments like this make me optimistic about the talent

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.080
that will carry exploration forward in the decades ahead.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.840
Missions that capture imaginations today

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.340
are quietly building the workforce of tomorrow.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.720
Speaking of inspiring young minds,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.320
a student team from Hawaii just earned national recognition.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.000
A project designed and led by undergraduates

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.720
from the University of Hawaii has advanced to become

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.160
a finalist in a national NASA competition.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.580
The team developed meaningful concepts

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.000
that could contribute to future agency missions.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.360
This achievement highlights how universities

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.800
are becoming important hubs of fresh thinking

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.020
for space exploration.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.340
It is exciting to see student-led work taken seriously

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:55.180
at this level.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.160
These programs give young innovators real pathways

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.580
to shape the missions that will fly in the years ahead.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.560
While students dream up future missions,

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.060
two major defense contracts show how SpaceX

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.640
is deepening its role in national security space.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.160
SpaceX has been awarded a $57 million contract

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.920
from the Pentagon to provide military space communication services.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.020
The company has also been integrated

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.300
into the Golden Dome Missile Defense Software Development Group.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.760
These moves expand its involvement

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.820
well beyond traditional launch and satellite services.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.880
It is interesting to watch how a company

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.980
that began with rockets is now woven

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.360
into critical national security programs.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.800
The breadth of its contributions keeps growing

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.580
in ways that few would have predicted a decade ago.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.240
Let's step away from hardware and contracts now

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.720
and look up because the sky is putting on a show this week.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.900
The Lerid meteor shower reaches its peak activity

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.840
between April 17 and 24.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:52.820
On April 24, Venus will pass just 0.8 degrees north of Uranus.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.840
That close pairing offers a nice target

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.440
for binoculars in the evening sky.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.520
These annual events give backyard sky watchers

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.820
reliable chances to catch natural fireworks

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.200
and planetary conjunctions.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.920
I always enjoy how something as simple as looking up

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.620
can connect us to the larger motions of the solar system.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.540
If the weather is clear where you are,

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.860
it is worth stepping outside to enjoy the show.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.180
From meteors streaking through our atmosphere

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.540
to views of our own planet,

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.660
NASA also shared a beautiful orbital image this week.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.120
The agency released a striking photograph

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.840
showing the diverse patchwork of small farms

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.260
in Yunlin County, Taiwan.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.740
Multiple crops can be seen growing

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.420
in close proximity from orbit.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:34.140
Images like this demonstrate how Earth observation satellites

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.480
help monitor land use changes and food production systems.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.480
It is remarkable how much detail we can gather

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.140
about human activity and agriculture

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.060
from hundreds of kilometers above.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.000
These observations give us a broader perspective

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.160
on how we manage resources here at home.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.500
Now, here is something that blew my mind when I looked into it.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:55.660
If you fired a spacecraft the size of a grand piano

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.120
toward Jupiter at 10 kilometers per second,

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:00.940
it could leave the solar system traveling faster

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.920
than 20 kilometers per second after one close encounter.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:05.580
During a gravity assist,

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.140
the spacecraft falls into the giant planet's

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:09.940
gravitational well and accelerates.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.780
At closest approach, it zips behind Jupiter

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.640
in the direction the planet is moving around the sun.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.440
Then it climbs back out of that gravity well,

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.980
having effectively borrowed some of the planet's

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:21.760
orbital momentum.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.780
Because Jupiter is so massive,

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.480
it barely notices the exchange,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.140
yet the spacecraft gains real speed.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.640
Voyager 2 used multiple slingshots like this

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.340
to reach velocities that let it cross the orbit of Neptune.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.920
The counterintuitive part is that we are essentially

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.700
stealing a tiny bit of a planet's kinetic energy

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.840
to fling our machines farther into the cosmos.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:42.400
Think about that.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.220
The next time you hear about a probe heading

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:45.480
to the outer solar system,

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.240
it is elegant physics that turns the solar system

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.320
itself into a kind of cosmic highway.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.520
Before we go, keep an eye on how the Artemis 2 data

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.280
continues to shape the next round of habitat designs

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.320
as those results are fully analyzed.

